<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  behavior_tree.xml (problem4)

  Deliberately the SIMPLEST possible reactive-redescend tree this
  theory can express: no obstacle geometry anywhere, exactly ONE
  reactive guard (BatteryOver, auto-derived into a trigger - see
  below), and ONE fallback with two plain branches. Built for the
  performance investigation into why the theory's exact inference
  blows up once a fallback_node's branching gets chained across two or
  more hops: this problem isolates that mechanism from every other
  variable (obstacle count, polygon-restriction geometry, guard style)
  that earlier testing already ruled out one at a time. ALSO used as
  the regression case for bt_to_prolog.py's automatic guard-trigger
  derivation (see that file's own module docstring, "CONTROL-FLOW
  GUARD DERIVATION") - neither MoveTo below writes a triggers="..."
  attribute at all.

  Fallback (plain, non-reactive - BatteryFallback):
    ReactiveSequence (branch 1, TryGoal - battery allows it):
      BatteryOver threshold=70 : only try the goal leg if battery is
        currently above 70%, checked FRESH each descent (a live state
        check, not history-based), so it correctly fails on its own
        once battery drops below 70% partway through branch 1's walk.
      PlanWith algorithm=straight goal=22.275;2.075
      MoveTo (no triggers="..." needed)
    Sequence (plain - branch 2: go home):
      PlanWith algorithm=straight goal=2.275;2.075
      MoveTo (no triggers="..." needed)

  Branch 1's own MoveTo gets collision/battery automatically (see
  bt_to_prolog.py), PLUS an automatically-DERIVED guard_break trigger
  from its own left sibling, BatteryOver threshold=70, tagged with the
  code of the SPECIFIC ReactiveSequence "TryGoal" it's a sibling of
  (translation assigns this the same way it always assigned a code to
  every ReactiveSequence/ReactiveFallback occurrence - nothing here is
  hand-picked). Firing it makes ONLY that ReactiveSequence restart its
  own children (fresh, from BatteryOver) rather than redescending the
  whole tree: BatteryOver now reads the CURRENT (already-below-70%)
  battery level and fails immediately, so TryGoal (still non-reactive
  as far as its own parent is concerned) simply fails, and the outer
  plain BatteryFallback does what a plain fallback always does - falls
  through to branch 2 (GoHome). Net effect is identical to the old
  whole-tree redescend for this particular tree shape (unlike
  problem3's obstacle_on_path, battery level is a simple state fluent
  that's always meaningfully checkable, live, with no "was there ever
  a walk in progress" precondition to work around), which is why this
  problem is also used as the regression check that localized
  redescend reproduces the prior global-redescend results.

  Start position (2.275,2.075, this problem's own config.yaml) IS the
  "home" this tree returns to; the goal (22.275,2.075) is 20 metres
  east of it, tuned together with config.yaml's raised
  moving_drain_rate (5.0%/time-unit, vs. problem0-3's shared 0.5) so
  the walk crosses the 70% threshold well before reaching the goal.

  A DistanceBelow node right after each branch's own MoveTo checks
  that leg's own noisy final position landed within 0.3m of whichever
  point that branch's own PlanWith targeted. See
  problem0/behavior_tree.xml's own header for the full rationale (this
  used to be baked into MoveTo's own status output, now an explicit
  condition node instead). Keeping this here (rather than dropping it,
  now that it's an opt-in tree-authoring choice) is what preserves
  this problem's own documented plan_outcome(true)/plan_outcome(false)
  split as a regression baseline.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Fallback name="BatteryFallback">
      <ReactiveSequence name="TryGoal">
        <BatteryOver threshold="70"/>
        <PlanWith algorithm="straight" goal="22.275;2.075" control_points="{cp}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp}"/>
        <DistanceBelow goal="22.275;2.075" threshold="0.3"/>
      </ReactiveSequence>
      <Sequence name="GoHome">
        <PlanWith algorithm="straight" goal="2.275;2.075" control_points="{cp2}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp2}"/>
        <DistanceBelow goal="2.275;2.075" threshold="0.3"/>
      </Sequence>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>

  <TreeNodesModel>
    <Action ID="MoveTo">
      <input_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <input_port name="triggers" type="std::vector&lt;std::string&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Action ID="PlanWith">
      <input_port name="algorithm" type="std::string"/>
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
      <input_port name="obstacle_id" type="std::string"/>
      <input_port name="offset" type="double"/>
      <output_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Condition ID="BatteryOver">
      <input_port name="threshold" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
    <Condition ID="DistanceBelow">
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
      <input_port name="threshold" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
  </TreeNodesModel>
</root>
